Context: the 1986 'Analysis situs (deuxième mouture)' develops an abstract combinatorial theory of 'figures', apparently aimed at the tame/stratified topology programme sketched in Esquisse d'un programme §5–6. Earlier pages define an abstract structure (𝔉, ≤, ≪). 𝔉 is a set of figures; ≤ is a refinement or containment order; ≪ is an adherence or 'is shadowed by' relation, required to be transitive (AE7 on p.27). To each figure F is attached a set Umb(F) ('ombre', shadow) of strata. Page 27 compares ≤ and ≪ in terms of inclusion Umb F ⊂ Umb G together with equivalence relations φ_F on these sets.

This page (Scholie) concludes that, via F ↦ Umb(F), the abstract structure (𝔉, ≤, ≪) is an axiomatization of the concrete notion Fig(M) of set-theoretic figures in a set M. The abstract data is therefore equivalent to:
- a set M;
- a subset 𝔉_M ⊂ Fig(M) ⊂ 𝔓(𝔓(M)), i.e. an element 𝔉_M ∈ 𝔓𝔓𝔓(M);
- subject to conditions to be written down 'tomorrow' as C1–C7.

This is a representation theorem: abstract structure = concrete family of families of subsets, in the style of Bourbaki échelons de structure. Grothendieck remarks that this gives renewed interest to a notion of '(grande) multiaxiomatique', which seems to mean structures specified by such iterated power-set data, possibly a 'large' one. He wonders whether it needs a terser notation and name.

He then announces axioms Ax*1, … phrased directly in terms of (M, 𝔉_M). Built in from the start: each element of 𝔉_M is a set of subsets of M which is a set-theoretic figure in M.

The next page gives Ax*1. Let M* be the union of all Φ ∈ 𝔉_M, i.e. the set of all strata. Ax*1 says:
- M* is itself a figure with support M;
- the associated preorder on M is an order (a T0 condition);
- x ↦ Omb(x) = M_{≪x} is a bijection M → M* turning ≪ into inclusion;
- the strata are exactly the irreducible closed subsets having a generic point.

So M is a poset/Alexandrov-type T0 space of 'elementary strata', and figures are certain families of such closures. This is a combinatorial model of stratifications.
